Date:     Tue, 16 Nov 93 6:37:54 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject:  Positive Feedback and Punishments

It occurs to me that the lack of positive feedback is the major problem
with e-mail lists, as well as newsgroups, for that matter.  No one
wants to waste time, so they don't post supportive messages unless
they think they have something new to add, but opposing posts get made
no matter what because those who disagree tend to think they have more
to add even when they don't.  Both HEX and the ratings schemes that have
cropeed up seem to attempts to solve this problem.

As for punishments, I think one of the problems is that the main form
of punishment has been posting bans.  Frankly, having been subject to
this myself, I don't think it's much of a deterrent.  I don't log in
every day, and I don't feel a need to post every time I do, so it's
no big deal if I'm forbidden to post for several days, even a week or
two.  But if I couldn't read, then...  In sum, I suggest that people
be punished by not being able to read the list, in those cases where
punishment is merited.  This has the added benefit of preventing the
punished from reading something they'd like to post in reaction to,
and being incensed to get around the rules in order to post it.

When it comes to "libelous" claims, I prefer the standard that the
only thing actionable be that which is known to be false by the one
who spreads the claim, not things whose truth or falsehood is
unknown.  Otherwise, just asking whether such a claim is true or
not would be libelous, and inhibit learning.

Further, I think this ought to be restricted to claims about list
members, not other parties such as the members of the board of
corporations and institutes.  It doesn't make sense for a
jurisdiction to punish offenses against non-clients.  If they
want protection, let 'em subcribe for it in some form.

Lastly, all I can say is that all this administrative business
is making me less willing to pay for my list subscription.  More
matter, less art!  as the Bard wrote.

