Date: Tue, 16 Nov 93 0:47:13 PST
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Oceania vs. Cyberspatial Libertaria

I also agree that there are many weaknesses with Oceania,
and have communicated my concerns on the Oceania list
(oceania@world.std.com).

"Cypherspace" is much easier to pioneer sooner than any
substantial independent piece of real estate.  Indeed,
it would be great to see Eric Klein turn his ample fund-raising
talent to raising money to help lay down the basic architecture
of cypherspace (integrated PGP, remailers, DC-nets, digital
cash, raid-proof PC's, et. al.), providing funding for
a least a couple of the many talented cypherpunk programmers
to pursue this work full time.  I would contribute both 
scarce money and not so scarce time to such an effort.
This focused charity segues in nicely to the $trillions ($10e12's)
worth of cypherspace-based businesses that will likely spring
up once the ball gets rolling.  This dwarfs the markets
for space and ocean colonization combined, and the start-up costs 
are much lower.

Even with crypto-anarchy, we will still have to reside in
and deal with the physical world.  Thus the basic goal of
establishing libertarian physical communities is sound.
Here are some basic design principles:

* A free independent jurisdiction is going to attract businesses 
exluded by other jurisdictions.  These businesses will provide a large 
source of funding for the basic real estate improvements, utilities, 
etc. but they will also increase the threat of violent intervention 
from outside.

* Thus defense is paramount.  Lack of numbers and firepower must be
more than made up for by mobility and stealth.

* A basic form to consider is a "temporary autonomous zone" (TAZ).
This would be like an extended rave or convention, but 
on a more ambitious scale:
	-- A nearly full set of basic utilities, food, medicine, 
	schools, offices, housing, and other services made available 
	on site, either via discrete convention hosts or mobil 
	apparatus.
	-- Location is unpredictable and announced only among
	a close-knit web of trust in cypherspace.
	-- Includes both partying and serious face to face business,
	conducted privately on site.
	-- A close-knit, common culture, always in touch in cyberspace
	and often in touch in the physical world in various unpredictable
	ways.
	-- Information businesses participating in a TAZ culture 
	would have a fixed cypherspace address (including e-mail, 
	fax, voice, etc.) independent of the physical rave locations.
	-- Physical businesses participatin in a TAZ culture would
	be those already mobile by nature (eg truckers), and those
	providing decentralized basic utilities, light manufacturing,
	physical services, etc. for the TAZ community.
	-- A TAZ lasts for weeks to years, depending on how it gets
	along with the locals.  No matter how long it lasts, TAZzers 
	must always be prepared to move overnight.
	-- Many TAZ's will be religious or ethnic in nature.

On the ocean, this might take the form of ongoing random excursions 
on discrete cruise lines.  (Has anybody priced these
lately?  I doubt Oceania can come in with a lower price/head
than a modern cruise ship).

On a smaller scale, many cypherspace inhabits may take up the
lifestyle of the "perpetual tourist" (PT), planting their flag
in some reasonably free country (perhaps even an Oceania with
rusting pontoons), and travelling/residing in Europe, the U.S.,
the Caribean, or wherever the business and parties are good.

