Date: Tue, 16 Nov 1993 08:02:06 -0600 (CST)
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: AIT: Question on algorithmic parsimony and GP

Phil Fraering:

> Can Genetic Programming be used to express any algorithm as a data set
> and an arbitrarily parsimonious GP algorithm?

Yes (for a reasonable meaning of "arbitrarily").

> Now the entire algorithm can be recreated without the data stream D()
> or the fitness function F() as long as the sequential output of the
> fitness function O(F()) is available and P() can be reset to where it
> was at the beginning of the algorithm.

This is fine; your GP + O(F()) + P() is a perfectly valid re-representation
of the algorithm.

> Is this idea of any use?

Unfortunately, probably not.  As you note, it is unlikely to afford
any actual compression.  The reason is that it is unlikely in the
extreme that the number of fitness cases (sizeof(O(F()))) grows
sub-linearly with the size of the generated program for a "useful"
fitness function like compression.

> Is it a mistake to think of the algorithm as a data
> set that can be represented by a smaller combination of algorithm and
> data set?

Not at all.  The blurring of the distinction between program and
data is a normal way of AIT thinking.  Another way of thinking about
why this particular method is unlikely to work is to consider what
programs + data will do the best compression job.  For your thought
experiment to work, it has to be the case that the GP algorithm
(in which we might as well include P()) plus the stream of fitness
results must be a good choice -- but given that these are such a
restricted choice from the space of all possible programs and data
streams, how likely is it to be the best or even good?

Another analysis:  GP will test a generation (size N) of programs,
then choose the new generation based on the N fitness values.
Since the new generation will in general only include components
of a fraction of the old generation (as the very unfit members of
the old generation will simply be thrown away), we should be able
to fruitfully compress the *choices* used in constructing the
new generation to a size rather smaller than N (probably on the
order of log(N) but that would be quite hard to prove -- in any
case, we can design a specialized GP designed for choice compression
in this way, though the Schema Theorem may not hold for this
new algorithm).  So a variant of your scheme in which the fitness
functions aren't stored, but just the "winners", would do even
better.

Not better enough, though, because we're still choosing from way
too sparse a subset of possible programs and data to hope for
anything approaching optimal compression.

derek
